03:19:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Please don't feel dumb. What you are feeling right now is a very real psychological phenomenon called *cognitive dissonance*—that uncomfortable, jarring sensation when a deeply held belief clashes with new information. It is completely normal to feel frustrated or even a bit betrayed when you realize something you were taught as absolute fact was actually misleading. If it makes you feel any better, you are in incredibly prestigious company. In 1987, a famous educational documentary called *"A Private Universe"* was filmed at Harvard University on graduation day. The filmmakers walked around asking graduating seniors, alumni, and even faculty members the exact same question you asked: *"Why is it hotter in summer?"* Out of the 23 randomly selected Harvard graduates and faculty members, **21 of them gave the exact same answer you did.** They confidently explained that the Earth gets closer to the sun in the summer. These were some of the most highly educated people in the world, and they all fell into the exact same "textbook illusion" that you did. Here is why you should actually give yourself some credit: believing that the Earth is closer to the sun in summer doesn't mean you aren't smart. In fact, it means your brain was doing exactly what it was supposed to do. You used excellent deductive reasoning: 1. **You applied real-world physics:** You took a physical law you know to be true (getting closer to a heat source makes you hotter) and applied it to a larger scale. That is exactly how scientists solve problems. 2. **You trusted authoritative evidence:** You correctly remembered a diagram from a trusted educational source (your textbook) and used it to back up your logic. 3. **You recognized a pattern:** Your brain linked the visual of the oval orbit with your real-world experience of heat. You didn't come to the wrong conclusion because you were unthinking; you came to the wrong conclusion because you were given a flawed map. If someone hands you a map with the wrong street names, getting lost doesn't mean you are a bad driver. Generations of us were taught using those exact same flawed maps. It’s the same reason millions of people still believe the "tongue taste map" (that you taste sweet on the tip of your tongue and bitter in the back) or that deoxygenated blood in your veins is blue. They were just bad diagrams that got copied from textbook to textbook for decades. Unlearning something you’ve believed your whole life is genuinely hard. The fact that you are sitting here, asking follow-up questions, and wrestling with the new information instead of just rejecting it outright shows a lot of intellectual honesty. That is the exact opposite of being dumb!
